⚠️ severity: IMPORTANT —
⚠️ **IMPORTANT: head-probe failure silently re-opens the #1410 approve path (fail-open in the new ladder)**

In `probeMachineVerdictMarker`, when trusted marker records exist but the head-sha probe fails, the marker becomes invisible and the legacy parser decides:

```js
var records = markerRecordsFromPayload(ctx.prCommentsPayload, logins);
if (!records.length) return null;
var headSha = ctx.pr ? headShaOf(io, opts.repo, ctx.pr) : '';   // transport failure → ''
ctx.headSha = headSha;
ctx.marker = markerForHead(records, headSha);                    // latestVerdictForHead('') → null
```

`markerForHead(records, '')` returns null (`latestVerdictForHead` bails on an empty sha), so `resolveEffectiveDecision(null, contract)` falls through to `source: 'legacy-parser'` — and a genuine `approve` contract then rides `applyApproveVerdict` → green checks → `pr_approved`, while a REQUEST_CHANGES marker from a trusted machine identity may sit on the current head.

The header's "marker invisible → legacy fallback" list enumerates four causes: pre-gh-807 shapes, **another head**, untrusted author, no configured identity. A **failed head probe** is none of them — in that case the marker exists for *some* head, quite possibly this one, and we failed to determine which. The ticket's own goal ("marker present → its verdict governs; no downgrade in any case") is exactly what this path defeats, and it needs only a transient `gh pr view --json headRefOid` failure (rate limit, network blip) coinciding with an approve-shaped parser contract — i.e. the #1410 pathology re-entering through the one branch the ladder doesn't guard.

Suggested fix (one guard, in this function):

```js
if (!records.length) return null;
var headSha = ctx.pr ? headShaOf(io, opts.repo, ctx.pr) : '';
if (ctx.pr && !headSha) {
    // trusted records exist but the head is unresolvable — never fail open
    // to the legacy parser: loud escalation (gh-305/gh-1419 treatment)
}
```

— either escalate needs-human there, or minimally force `parserVerdictClassOf` to never classify `approve` while unresolvable trusted records exist (rework is the safe side; approve still arrives on a re-run once gh recovers).

Also note the AC test set covers "marker for a different head sha" (AC1g) but not "probe fails" — that case is the gap and deserves a pinned test alongside the fix.

